{:edb/db-before-t 1485 :edb/db-after-t 1486 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534798 50 #inst "2026-10-08T07:16:48.513Z" 13194139534798 true] [17592186096374 1100 "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" 13194139534798 true] [17592186096374 1100 "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/bfa592bb1f4e5a292e1e94476ab6e5f1.png)" 13194139534798 false] [17592186096374 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$" 13194139534798 true] [17592186096374 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/49fbbe1f5dd4731054af33666b88cb64.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2 \\mid \\mathbf{n} \\mid$;\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2 \\mid \\mathbf{k} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ON^(→) + NM^(→) \\\\\n&= 2n + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$" 13194139534798 false] [17592186096375 1100 "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/8512f8ab1660315b7e5f0aa8beb302ff.png)" 13194139534798 false] [17592186096375 1100 "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b},$ and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/678ea2936b8a5b1ded74cb8a0875c629e8be0f7002c98492ebf2e8109fc603ef.png)" 13194139534798 true] [17592186096375 1145 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nso we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}$.\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= AO^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-OA^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-a + b \\\\\n&= b - a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOX^(→) &= OA^(→) + AX^(→) \\\\\n&= a + \\frac{3}{4}(b - a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}a + \\frac{3}{4}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534798 false] [17592186096375 1145 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534798 true] [17592186096418 1100 "Consider the picture below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/f2518c2e998e7c7f551548a4cc7283720a66428ea8fa9872494a81cc3848ba9e.png)\n\nGiven that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/c6714d0a6eaa5c7d354d46e8d6d60da7f3d2011df0fc75773036cf7ae510c5e3.png), find ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/24878591902884f45f14f5c447bc326586a411a8870b205fc12825a4ab2c738a.png)" 13194139534798 true] [17592186096418 1100 "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/c2827bf97f6d17572e9ac2b808d16a69.png)" 13194139534798 false] [17592186096418 1145 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/0b673cb49bd6ea0955e57952763b7458.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup OAC$, we have:\n\