R}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310765 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310766 13194139534798 true] [17592186310765 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534798 true] [17592186310765 1041 "field-1" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310765 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310766 13194139534798 true] [17592186310765 1110 #uuid "2350f5b4-d483-51b9-81f0-c41ce94c8b0e" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310766 1034 "1" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310766 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534798 true] [17592186310766 1111 #uuid "a66462c1-78ff-5d0c-8e9d-7113f04b9388" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310767 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310768 13194139534798 true] [17592186310767 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534798 true] [17592186310767 1041 "field-2" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310767 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310768 13194139534798 true] [17592186310767 1110 #uuid "535ea4ed-4ac4-537c-ae91-4ecc883b64f9" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310768 1034 "4" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310768 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534798 true] [17592186310768 1111 #uuid "a515de7a-dcd0-5b10-a8b9-7f93b67111c2" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310769 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310770 13194139534798 true] [17592186310769 1089 "q-327049" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310769 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534798 true] [17592186310769 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/a43139fc26d5778c40db020a7ebc9122c2fd54c0dcd4eb44cfd1671936a4055a.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a+2b,$ then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534798 true] [17592186310769 1113 #uuid "6784d6a1-7329-59ce-9957-b8288b4233b2" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310769 1145 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041137/assets/1d266a8324d18191f5bc8560be6539d54d1a11916f33ead7432d9f528769fc22.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=\\frac{1}{2}a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310771 13194139534798 true] [17592186310770 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310772 13194139534798 true] [17592186310770 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534798 true] [17592186310770 1041 "field-1" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310770 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310771 13194139534798 true] [17592186310770 1110 #uuid "456f1dcc-ef20-59d2-a8c8-6997a0ea7edc" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310771 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310771 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534798 true] [17592186310771 1111 #uuid "91e879d6-45f0-55a9-a966-627d6a5a481b" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310772 1034 "1" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310772 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534798 true] [17592186310772 1111 #uuid "57a5bc70-f8a6-537e-beb8-e1db288ff4ba" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310773 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310774 13194139534798 true] [17592186310773 1089 "q-70149" 13194139534798 true] [17592186310773 1091 #edb/